#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01eb53d6-55a8-542f-8163-5afe82f78270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250571} {:answer/id #uuid "508d017e-b033-5327-85e5-ab0274ce911f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251213} {:answer/id #uuid "a05ad30a-1ed4-55d8-8378-b7dbf575700d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251936} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf1fd11-1928-5041-b1c9-06fb7edc1519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252212} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc73ce0-d6f2-58f7-9cee-512addcd38fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbf1fd11-1928-5041-b1c9-06fb7edc1519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252212} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc56aa86-a6fb-55a8-8dfc-3acc8e9fec8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d647eaf0-9519-521e-a229-1bff754ddbe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-117702" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f725b571954587b7b88426a254d7a6fc05f09b63ab2cc94b81303a510e8c1798.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in [0,2),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [2,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,3].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =bh \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-2+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84dcf942-5a22-5fad-824e-f3fa9cfe7925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84dcf942-5a22-5fad-824e-f3fa9cfe7925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309782} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb50a4f5-9a86-5be7-b5dc-d05a6bfd5893" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c24171a6-04c2-51af-8603-621f0f04a134" :question/math-academy-id "q-338331" :question/problem "![](images/06/06ace831b877406e48fe8f1ab38a7244e7ade878d7c77b99d8230f40ad307207.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-3],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-3,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,2],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (2,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}3(3) \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)(2) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (2,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}3(6) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =4.5+3+6+9 \\\\  & =22.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae9f34e-7db8-57fc-94c6-4949ee0d2b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi -8}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ae9f34e-7db8-57fc-94c6-4949ee0d2b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi -8}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312305} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8642157-99dd-5a81-9c8e-ea0475be9d9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d781642-b0e7-55aa-9dff-ba9e64f64167" :question/math-academy-id "q-339677" :question/problem "![](images/35/353412402972b390de950f56b3273d8dbecf60d4d07c7c27e9281c28145a86d2.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [-3,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (1,4],$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [4,7].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-1].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a rectangle and a quarter-circle of radius $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(3)-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=\\pi -6.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,7].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle of radius $3,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =\\pi -6-2+6+\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{13\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi -8}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b429cbf-699c-5402-ba85-c66f431ab290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330047} {:answer/id #uuid "69f7df2a-712e-5d88-8c8c-2c5d26706731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330900} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a1bf43-831a-54f4-9b93-23a8155c13c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331432} {:answer/id #uuid "b19a89e7-a647-5d27-a40a-536fedc46b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331520} {:answer/id #uuid "d705ef81-b0f1-56a0-88ab-9f7f04d8033f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b429cbf-699c-5402-ba85-c66f431ab290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330047} :answer-field/id #uuid "1acaa5f4-8d2b-5e14-a84d-f463ca575c5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf1250a6-33da-5c83-b34e-994a1715d47c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49169" :question/problem "![](images/55/555b702c1903f8cc4e379ad4281aa86708757755e4f9fb14b0ec7790a80a17dd.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2),$ the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (-2,0],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2).$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle with the radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle with the radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$  The corresponding region is the trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2+4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-12.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\pi +\\pi -12=2\\pi -12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
